WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.185
Lesson 4.1.

00:00:02.185 --> 00:00:05.371
Yield farming: base yield vs emissions.

00:00:05.371 --> 00:00:07.687
By the end, you'll be able to split

00:00:07.687 --> 00:00:11.224
any farm's advertised APY into what's actually earned,

00:00:11.224 --> 00:00:14.183
and what's simply printed, and answer the one

00:00:14.183 --> 00:00:16.756
question that actually matters about it.

00:00:18.056 --> 00:00:19.346
Here's the plan.

00:00:19.346 --> 00:00:22.801
Base yield, what's genuinely earned from real activity.

00:00:22.801 --> 00:00:25.641
Emissions, what's simply printed and handed out.

00:00:25.641 --> 00:00:28.132
Mercenary liquidity, the capital that only

00:00:28.132 --> 00:00:30.286
ever showed up for the printing.

00:00:30.286 --> 00:00:34.035
And a full worked example, using the source material's

00:00:34.035 --> 00:00:37.376
own sixty percent A.P.Y. farm, split all the way

00:00:37.376 --> 00:00:40.171
down to the number that actually matters.

00:00:41.471 --> 00:00:43.486
Base yield vs. emissions.

00:00:44.786 --> 00:00:47.207
Here's exactly where a farming A.P.Y.

00:00:47.207 --> 00:00:50.501
splits, underneath that single headline number.

00:00:50.501 --> 00:00:53.860
Base yield is trading fees, or borrower interest,

00:00:53.860 --> 00:00:56.815
paid by real users doing real activity, the

00:00:56.815 --> 00:00:59.301
same kind of yield you'd find in any

00:00:59.301 --> 00:01:02.727
lending market or A.M.M. Emissions are new tokens,

00:01:02.727 --> 00:01:06.018
printed directly by the protocol, and handed out

00:01:06.018 --> 00:01:09.811
specifically to attract deposits it wants right now.

00:01:09.811 --> 00:01:11.798
Both get added together into the one

00:01:11.798 --> 00:01:13.946
number the dashboard actually shows you.

00:01:15.246 --> 00:01:18.849
Here's the actual mechanism behind why emissions

00:01:18.849 --> 00:01:21.570
A.P.Y.s tend to fall over time, even

00:01:21.570 --> 00:01:24.885
when nothing else about the farm changes.

00:01:24.885 --> 00:01:28.250
More capital arrives, chasing the advertised rate.

00:01:28.250 --> 00:01:30.949
That capital shares the same fixed pool of

00:01:30.949 --> 00:01:35.014
reward tokens, so each depositor's individual share shrinks.

00:01:35.014 --> 00:01:38.150
And separately, as more of the reward token gets

00:01:38.150 --> 00:01:41.350
printed and sold, its own market price very often

00:01:41.350 --> 00:01:44.806
declines, shrinking the real value of that share even

00:01:44.806 --> 00:01:46.854
further, on top of the dilution.

00:01:48.154 --> 00:01:51.665
Worth being precise about what emissions actually are, since

00:01:51.665 --> 00:01:55.189
the word can sound negative when it isn't automatically.

00:01:55.189 --> 00:01:58.212
Emissions are a cost, deliberately paid in the

00:01:58.212 --> 00:02:01.427
protocol's own token, to buy deposits faster than

00:02:01.427 --> 00:02:04.428
organic activity alone would attract them.

00:02:04.428 --> 00:02:07.465
That can be a completely reasonable strategy, for

00:02:07.465 --> 00:02:11.228
a protocol genuinely trying to bootstrap early liquidity.

00:02:11.228 --> 00:02:14.109
The only mistake is treating that printed cost

00:02:14.109 --> 00:02:16.438
as if it were durable, ordinary yield.

00:02:17.738 --> 00:02:19.860
Here's how to actually find this split

00:02:19.860 --> 00:02:22.364
yourself, when a farm's own dashboard doesn't

00:02:22.364 --> 00:02:24.133
show it broken out already.

00:02:24.133 --> 00:02:27.458
Check the underlying pool's trading volume against its

00:02:27.458 --> 00:02:30.602
total value locked, since that ratio, combined with

00:02:30.602 --> 00:02:33.081
the pool's fee rate, gives you a genuine

00:02:33.081 --> 00:02:35.013
estimate of the base yield.

00:02:35.013 --> 00:02:38.583
Separately, check the protocol's emissions dashboard or

00:02:38.583 --> 00:02:41.708
documentation for exactly how many reward tokens

00:02:41.708 --> 00:02:44.303
are allocated to that specific pool.

00:02:44.303 --> 00:02:47.390
And treat whatever's left over, once base yield is

00:02:47.390 --> 00:02:51.143
subtracted from the headline number, as the emissions portion.

00:02:52.443 --> 00:02:53.533
Quick check.

00:02:53.533 --> 00:02:55.373
What's the actual test for whether

00:02:55.373 --> 00:02:56.688
a farm is worth the risk?

00:03:01.108 --> 00:03:04.583
The answer: does the base yield alone, with emissions

00:03:04.583 --> 00:03:08.058
removed entirely, justify the risk you'd be taking on.

00:03:09.358 --> 00:03:10.733
Mercenary liquidity.

00:03:12.033 --> 00:03:14.965
Here's the actual sequence mercenary liquidity

00:03:14.965 --> 00:03:17.448
follows, from arrival to departure.

00:03:17.448 --> 00:03:21.257
It arrives specifically chasing an attractive emissions rate, not

00:03:21.257 --> 00:03:24.788
because it has any particular interest in that protocol.

00:03:24.788 --> 00:03:26.985
It stays for exactly as long as that

00:03:26.985 --> 00:03:30.788
rate remains attractive relative to alternatives elsewhere.

00:03:30.788 --> 00:03:33.973
And the moment incentives are reduced, or end, it

00:03:33.973 --> 00:03:37.222
leaves, often within days, taking a large share of

00:03:37.222 --> 00:03:40.153
the pool's total liquidity with it as it goes.

00:03:41.453 --> 00:03:44.059
Worth being precise about the actual damage

00:03:44.059 --> 00:03:46.487
this does to whoever stays behind, since

00:03:46.487 --> 00:03:49.333
it isn't just that the emissions disappear.

00:03:49.333 --> 00:03:52.357
When a large share of a pool's liquidity leaves

00:03:52.357 --> 00:03:55.191
all at once, trading in that pool often gets

00:03:55.191 --> 00:03:58.782
thinner and more volatile too, meaning even the genuine,

00:03:58.782 --> 00:04:01.994
organic base yield can fall for the depositors who

00:04:01.994 --> 00:04:05.018
remain, on top of losing the emissions entirely.

00:04:06.318 --> 00:04:10.532
Here's the actual difference between capital that's mercenary and

00:04:10.532 --> 00:04:13.725
capital that isn't, side by side, since not every

00:04:13.725 --> 00:04:16.643
depositor in a farm behaves the same way.

00:04:16.643 --> 00:04:20.773
Mercenary capital tracks the emissions rate specifically, and moves

00:04:20.773 --> 00:04:23.988
the instant a better rate appears anywhere else.

00:04:23.988 --> 00:04:26.973
Capital that's there for the base yield itself tends

00:04:26.973 --> 00:04:30.014
to stay through emissions changes, since it was never

00:04:30.014 --> 00:04:32.436
the reason it showed up in the first place.

00:04:33.736 --> 00:04:34.826
Quick check.

00:04:34.826 --> 00:04:37.151
What is mercenary liquidity, exactly?

00:04:41.571 --> 00:04:44.174
The answer: capital that moves on as soon

00:04:44.174 --> 00:04:47.024
as incentives end, often taking a large share

00:04:47.024 --> 00:04:49.441
of the pool's base yield along with it.

00:04:50.741 --> 00:04:51.746
Worked example.

00:04:53.046 --> 00:04:55.911
Here's the farm, exactly as advertised.

00:04:55.911 --> 00:04:59.701
A dashboard shows sixty percent A.P.Y., total.

00:04:59.701 --> 00:05:02.829
Split honestly, that's five percent from genuine

00:05:02.829 --> 00:05:05.765
trading fees, and fifty-five percent paid out

00:05:05.765 --> 00:05:08.044
in reward tokens, as emissions.

00:05:08.044 --> 00:05:11.884
Already, before anything else happens, more than ninety percent

00:05:11.884 --> 00:05:15.424
of that headline number is emissions, not organic activity.

00:05:16.724 --> 00:05:19.764
Now here's what happens once you account for the

00:05:19.764 --> 00:05:23.548
reward token's actual price, using the source material's own

00:05:23.548 --> 00:05:27.457
realistic assumption: that it falls fifty percent, on average,

00:05:27.457 --> 00:05:31.479
across the period you're actually harvesting and selling it.

00:05:31.479 --> 00:05:34.395
Fifty-five percent of emissions, valued at half

00:05:34.395 --> 00:05:36.886
price, is worth about twenty-seven and a

00:05:36.886 --> 00:05:39.494
half percent, not the full fifty-five.

00:05:39.494 --> 00:05:41.657
Add that back to the five percent in

00:05:41.657 --> 00:05:44.170
genuine fees, and the real return comes to

00:05:44.170 --> 00:05:46.274
about thirty-two and a half percent.

00:05:47.574 --> 00:05:50.872
And here's the actual question this whole exercise

00:05:50.872 --> 00:05:53.759
exists to answer, laid out side by side.

00:05:53.759 --> 00:05:57.972
With emissions included, even realistically discounted, the farm still

00:05:57.972 --> 00:06:01.239
shows an attractive thirty-two and a half percent.

00:06:01.239 --> 00:06:04.175
But strip emissions out completely, and account

00:06:04.175 --> 00:06:07.234
for nothing except genuine, durable activity, and

00:06:07.234 --> 00:06:09.559
you're left with exactly five percent.

00:06:10.859 --> 00:06:13.593
Here's the one question this entire worked

00:06:13.593 --> 00:06:16.008
example exists to make you ask, every

00:06:16.008 --> 00:06:18.724
single time, before entering any farm.

00:06:18.724 --> 00:06:21.328
Would you take on this specific protocol's

00:06:21.328 --> 00:06:24.173
risk, its smart contract risk, its impermanent

00:06:24.173 --> 00:06:26.894
loss, its gas costs, for five percent?

00:06:26.894 --> 00:06:29.119
Not for the sixty percent headline.

00:06:29.119 --> 00:06:30.964
Not even for the thirty-two and

00:06:30.964 --> 00:06:33.109
a half percent realistic number.

00:06:33.109 --> 00:06:35.504
For the five percent that's actually durable.

00:06:36.804 --> 00:06:38.769
How sensitive is that number, really?

00:06:40.069 --> 00:06:43.535
Here's the exact same farm, run across five different

00:06:43.535 --> 00:06:47.193
assumptions for how far the reward token actually falls,

00:06:47.193 --> 00:06:50.125
from a full recovery to a total collapse.

00:06:50.125 --> 00:06:52.859
If the reward token doesn't fall at all, the

00:06:52.859 --> 00:06:56.015
real return is the full sixty percent headline.

00:06:56.015 --> 00:06:59.975
At a twenty-five percent decline, it's about forty-six percent.

00:06:59.975 --> 00:07:01.825
At the source material's own fifty

00:07:01.825 --> 00:07:04.345
percent assumption, thirty-two and a half.

00:07:04.345 --> 00:07:07.035
At seventy-five percent, under nineteen.

00:07:07.035 --> 00:07:09.774
And if the reward token falls to zero, all

00:07:09.774 --> 00:07:12.832
that's left is the five percent in genuine fees.

00:07:14.132 --> 00:07:17.727
Notice how this line is almost perfectly straight, which

00:07:17.727 --> 00:07:21.385
tells you something important: the entire result rides on

00:07:21.385 --> 00:07:24.917
a single assumption, how much the reward token actually

00:07:24.917 --> 00:07:29.317
falls, and that assumption drives the outcome almost one-for-one.

00:07:29.317 --> 00:07:31.939
Guess a smaller decline than what actually

00:07:31.939 --> 00:07:35.049
happens, and you've overestimated your real return

00:07:35.049 --> 00:07:36.757
by roughly that same margin.

00:07:38.057 --> 00:07:41.222
Here's how to actually estimate that decline yourself,

00:07:41.222 --> 00:07:43.652
for a real farm, instead of guessing.

00:07:43.652 --> 00:07:46.235
Pull up the reward token's own price chart,

00:07:46.235 --> 00:07:49.113
and look specifically at how it's behaved during

00:07:49.113 --> 00:07:52.407
past emissions periods, not just its all-time high.

00:07:52.407 --> 00:07:55.365
Check the emission schedule itself, since a faster

00:07:55.365 --> 00:07:58.217
printing rate tends to mean faster dilution.

00:07:58.217 --> 00:08:01.133
And when genuinely unsure, default to the more

00:08:01.133 --> 00:08:04.422
conservative estimate, since a farm that clears that

00:08:04.422 --> 00:08:06.532
bar clears the optimistic one too.

00:08:07.832 --> 00:08:08.922
Quick check.

00:08:08.922 --> 00:08:12.447
Why do emissions A.P.Y.s tend to fall over time?

00:08:16.867 --> 00:08:20.117
The answer: more capital arrives to share the same

00:08:20.117 --> 00:08:23.430
reward pool, and the reward token's own price often

00:08:23.430 --> 00:08:26.297
declines as more of it gets printed and sold.

00:08:27.597 --> 00:08:29.092
Setting your harvest schedule.

00:08:30.392 --> 00:08:33.634
Worth deciding this specific detail before you ever

00:08:33.634 --> 00:08:37.002
deposit, not while you're already watching the reward

00:08:37.002 --> 00:08:39.297
token's price move in real time.

00:08:39.297 --> 00:08:42.466
A fixed schedule, say harvesting and converting to

00:08:42.466 --> 00:08:44.765
a stablecoin on a set day each week,

00:08:44.765 --> 00:08:47.997
removes the temptation to hold reward tokens hoping

00:08:47.997 --> 00:08:50.669
for a recovery that the earlier chart just

00:08:50.669 --> 00:08:52.657
showed you isn't the way to bet.

00:08:53.957 --> 00:08:55.417
Here's your checklist.

00:08:55.417 --> 00:08:58.227
Do it before entering any farm, not after.

00:08:58.227 --> 00:09:01.995
Split the advertised A.P.Y. into base yield versus

00:09:01.995 --> 00:09:06.506
incentives, explicitly, using the protocol's own numbers.

00:09:06.506 --> 00:09:09.607
Value the reward token at a realistic price, one

00:09:09.607 --> 00:09:13.451
that assumes it falls, not its current, best-case price.

00:09:13.451 --> 00:09:16.348
And set your harvest-and-sell schedule in advance,

00:09:16.348 --> 00:09:18.848
rather than deciding it emotionally, in the

00:09:18.848 --> 00:09:21.461
moment, while watching the token's price move.

00:09:22.761 --> 00:09:24.636
The same method, a different farm.

00:09:25.936 --> 00:09:28.988
Here's the same method, applied to a completely

00:09:28.988 --> 00:09:32.486
different, purely illustrative farm, just to show it's

00:09:32.486 --> 00:09:35.729
a repeatable habit, not a one-off calculation tied

00:09:35.729 --> 00:09:38.509
to that specific sixty percent example.

00:09:38.509 --> 00:09:41.191
Say a farm shows eighty percent A.P.Y.:

00:09:41.191 --> 00:09:44.441
ten percent fees, seventy percent emissions.

00:09:44.441 --> 00:09:47.307
At the same realistic fifty percent decline

00:09:47.307 --> 00:09:50.889
assumption, that's forty-five percent real return, and

00:09:50.889 --> 00:09:54.381
just ten percent with emissions removed entirely.

00:09:54.381 --> 00:09:55.626
Same method.

00:09:55.626 --> 00:09:58.681
Same honest question, on a completely different number.

00:09:59.981 --> 00:10:01.226
Let's recap.

00:10:01.226 --> 00:10:02.998
Base yield comes from real

00:10:02.998 --> 00:10:05.726
activity: fees and borrower interest.

00:10:05.726 --> 00:10:07.775
Emissions are printed tokens, a

00:10:07.775 --> 00:10:10.316
deliberate cost, not durable yield.

00:10:10.316 --> 00:10:13.140
Emissions A.P.Y.s tend to fall, through

00:10:13.140 --> 00:10:16.476
dilution and falling token price, together.

00:10:16.476 --> 00:10:20.200
Mercenary liquidity arrives for the emissions and leaves the

00:10:20.200 --> 00:10:23.491
moment they end, often taking base yield with it.

00:10:23.491 --> 00:10:26.591
And the real question is never the headline number:

00:10:26.591 --> 00:10:29.571
it's what's left once emissions are gone entirely.

00:10:30.871 --> 00:10:33.944
Before entering any farm, split its APY, value

00:10:33.944 --> 00:10:37.343
the reward token realistically, and ask whether the

00:10:37.343 --> 00:10:39.931
base yield alone is worth the risk.

00:10:39.931 --> 00:10:42.956
Next up, Lesson 4.2: native staking.
